This function uses recursion to find the greatest common divisor of two positive integers. It works by repeatedly calling itself with the smaller number as the first argument and the larger number as the second argument, until it reaches a base case where the second argument is 0. In that case, the function returns the first argument, which is the greatest common divisor.

For example, if we call `gcd(12, 15)`, the function will first call `gcd(12, 15 - 12)` which is `gcd(12, 3)`. Since 3 is not 0, the function will again call `gcd(3, 12 - 3)` which is `gcd(3, 9)`. Since 9 is not 0, the function will again call `gcd(9, 12 - 9)` which is `gcd(9, 3)`. Since 3 is 0, the function will return 9, which is the greatest common divisor of 12 and 15.

